  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will build east across the region through today. \nClouds will clear out from NW to SE early this morning, with \nsunny skies and slightly below normal temperatures this \nafternoon. With high pressure directly over the area tonight, \nlows will be cool ranging from upper 30s in the Adirondacks to \nnear 50 in the mid Hudson Valley. Patchy/areas of fog will occur\nin typical sheltered valleys and near warmer bodies of water.\n\nThe high will shift east off the New England coast on Sat, with\na warmer southerly flow developing. So after a cool start to \nthe day, temperatures will warm to normal level in the \nafternoon. Along with warmer temperatures, moisture will also \nrapidly increase with PWAT anomalies increasing to +1 to +3 \nSTDEV Sat night(greatest from ALB south). This coincides with a \nshort wave disturbance moving into the area from the south/west \nand a developing surface cyclone near NYC. With the increase in \nmoisture and forcing, widespread showers are now expected Sat \nnight into Sun morning. While instability is lacking, deep layer\nmoisture increase will result in localized downpours. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a brief break, isolated to widely scattered thunderstorms\nare expected to develop Sun afternoon ahead of a potent upper \nlevel short wave and associated surface cold front. Guidance \nindicating moderate instability developing, with 1000-1500 J/Kg \nSBCAPE across eastern NY, with < 1000 J/Kg in western New \nEngland. 0-6 km shear also increases to around 35-45 kt. The \nCAPE/shear balance may result in organized storms, with some \nbecoming strong to severe. The threat for storms will end Sun \nnight, as a drier and cooler air mass builds in by Mon morning.",
